The Security Council,

Guided by the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations, in particular the commitment to maintain international peace and security, develop friendly relations among nations based on respect for the principle of equal rights and self-determination of peoples, and achieve international cooperation in solving international problems,

Reaffirming its primary responsibility under the Charter for the maintenance of international peace and security,

Emphasizing the imperative of strict adherence to international law, including the principles of sovereign equality, territorial integrity, political independence of States, non-intervention in internal affairs, and the prohibition of the threat or use of force against any State,

Recalling the obligation of all Member States under Article 33 of the Charter to seek the pacific settlement of disputes through negotiation, enquiry, mediation, conciliation, arbitration, judicial settlement, resort to regional agencies or arrangements, or other peaceful means of their own choice,

Reaffirming that multilateralism and diplomacy are the cornerstone of the international order and essential for the peaceful resolution of conflicts, prevention of hostilities, and preservation of strategic stability,

Recognizing the indispensable role of the United Nations, and in particular the Security Council, the General Assembly, the International Court of Justice, and the Secretary-General, in facilitating the peaceful settlement of disputes and promoting preventive diplomacy,

Underlining the necessity of addressing the root causes of armed conflicts, including foreign occupation, denial of the right of self-determination, poverty, economic inequality, and violations of international humanitarian law and human rights,

Reiterating that all resolutions and decisions of the Security Council must be implemented faithfully and without selectivity, in accordance with Article 25 of the Charter,

1. Reaffirms its unwavering commitment to multilateralism, with the United Nations at its core, as the most effective framework to prevent and peacefully resolve international conflicts and address global challenges;

2. Calls upon all Member States to strictly comply with their obligations under the Charter of the United Nations and international law, and to refrain from the threat or use of force in international relations;

3. Urges all parties to existing and potential disputes to exhaust all peaceful means provided in Chapter VI of the Charter, including early engagement in dialogue, negotiation, and mediation, to resolve their differences amicably and in accordance with international law;

4. Emphasizes the need for the full, effective, and non-selective implementation of all relevant Security Council resolutions, particularly those addressing unresolved disputes and situations of foreign occupation, to ensure lasting peace and stability;

5. Reaffirms the universal right of peoples to self-determination, especially those living under colonial rule or foreign occupation, as recognized in the Charter of the United Nations and relevant United Nations resolutions, as a fundamental condition for the peaceful settlement of disputes;

6. Encourages the Secretary-General to continue utilizing the tools available under the Charter, particularly his good offices and preventive diplomacy, including under Article 99, to avert the outbreak or escalation of disputes and facilitate their peaceful resolution;

7. Highlights the crucial role of the International Court of Justice, as the principal judicial organ of the United Nations, in promoting the rule of law and resolving legal disputes submitted by States, and encourages Member States to make fuller use of its advisory opinions and contentious jurisdiction;

8. Underlines the importance of addressing the underlying drivers of conflict, including socioeconomic disparities, injustice, human rights violations, and impunity, through comprehensive and sustained peacebuilding and development initiatives;

9. Stresses the necessity of enhancing cooperation between the United Nations and regional and subregional organizations, in accordance with Chapter VIII of the Charter, to reinforce conflict prevention and peaceful settlement efforts, while respecting national sovereignty;

10. Requests the Secretary-General to submit a report within six months on concrete recommendations to strengthen the United Nations system’s capacity for preventive diplomacy, mediation, and the pacific settlement of disputes;

11. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

